content.thinking:
Confirmed state: two children (group-split-routing, push-allreduce) failed immediately with a provider 400 (no body) at launch; the workflow engine then stopped the other four ("Subagent stopped by user" — engine-initiated stop cascading from the runs.all rejection). Nothing completed, nothing was written. All runs are non-resumable (stopped runs not resumable; failed ones trivial).

Recovery: relaunch a new workflow with all six candidates, but this time use Promise.allSettled-style handling so one child's failure doesn't kill the rest. Also the 400s were instant — possibly transient provider errors. Two of six hit 400 immediately. To be safer, I could add a retry per child within the script: wrap each run in a helper that catches failure and retries once with a fresh key. That handles transient 400s without failing the whole workflow.

Let me write the workflow script with a retry-once wrapper using Promise.allSettled.

I'll reuse the same task texts. Keys: append "-r2" for retries to avoid same-key reuse conflicts (same-key reuse requires identical launch params — retry with new key is cleaner).

Script sketch:

```js
const candidates = [...six [key, task] pairs...];
async function runWithRetry(key, task) {
  try {
    const r = await runs.run(key, { agent: 'worker', task });
    return { key, status: 'ok', output: r.output };
  } catch (e) {
    const r2 = await runs.run(key + '-retry', { agent: 'worker', task });
    return { key, status: 'ok-after-retry', output: r2.output };
  }
}
const results = await Promise.all(candidates.map(([k, t]) => runWithRetry(k, t).catch(e => ({ key: k, status: 'failed', error: String(e) }))));
return results;
```

This way no single failure kills others, and transient 400s get one retry.

